Here \(f(x) = x + \frac{1}{x}\), so
\(f'(x) = 1 - \frac{1}{x^2} = \frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2}\).
Since \(x^2 > 0\) throughout the domain, the SIGN of \(f'\) is the sign of \(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\). It vanishes at \(x = 1\) and \(x = -1\), and these two points, together with the excluded point \(x = 0\), cut the domain into four pieces:
for \(x < -1\): \(x^2 > 1\), so \(f'(x) > 0\);
for \(-1 < x < 0\): \(x^2 < 1\), so \(f'(x) < 0\);
for \(0 < x < 1\): \(x^2 < 1\), so \(f'(x) < 0\);
for \(x > 1\): \(x^2 > 1\), so \(f'(x) > 0\).
Hence f is increasing on \((-\infty, -1]\) and on \([1, \infty)\) — that is, f is always increasing on \(R - (-1, 1)\) — and it is decreasing on \((-1, 0)\) and on \((0, 1)\).
(The endpoints \(\pm 1\) may be included because \(f'\) vanishes only at those isolated points; \(x = -1\) gives the local maximum \(f(-1) = -2\) and \(x = 1\) gives the local minimum \(f(1) = 2\).)
